## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical batch ingestion, per-record contracts, privacy masking, and idempotent upserts in a common lakehouse pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts, masking, bad_data routing
- Iceberg schema evolution, upserts, unique keys
- Test plans for duplicates, new fields, and schema drift

## Code Example

```javascript
function validate(record) {
  const required = ["user_id","event_ts","event_type"];
  for (const k of required) if (!(k in record)) return false;
  // simple mask
  if (record.payload) record.payload.pii = null;
  return true;
}
```

```javascript
// MERGE-like upsert (pseudocode)
MERGE INTO iceberg.fact AS t
USING staging AS s
ON t.user_id = s.user_id AND t.event_ts = s.event_ts
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ...
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ...
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

